WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.734
-Our first guest tonight
is a Golden Globe-winning

00:00:02.734 --> 00:00:04.934
and Academy Award-nominated
actress you know best from

00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:07.801
"Almost Famous" and "How to Lose
a Guy in 10 Days."

00:00:07.801 --> 00:00:11.300
She stars in the second season
of the Apple TV+ series

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.434
"Truth Be Told," with new
episodes streaming on Fridays.

00:00:14.434 --> 00:00:15.868
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.868 --> 00:00:19.467
-You're the closest thing
I've ever had to a mother, so...

00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:28.167
-Sweetie.

00:00:30.934 --> 00:00:33.701
I miss him so much.

00:00:33.701 --> 00:00:35.934
-Me too.

00:00:35.934 --> 00:00:38.701
-I really don't like
when people lie to me.

00:00:42.234 --> 00:00:44.434
-Please welcome back
to the show and the studio

00:00:44.434 --> 00:00:46.200
Kate Hudson, everybody.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.534
♪♪

00:00:47.534 --> 00:00:50.167
Hi.

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:53.167
Nice to see you.
-Hi!

00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:55.501
Good to see you.
I can't believe this.

00:00:55.501 --> 00:00:57.634
-Right? It's nice.
It's real human beings.

00:00:57.634 --> 00:01:01.467
-It's like -- This is my first
back in the studio --

00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:02.901
-It's your first studio
talk show in a while.

00:01:02.901 --> 00:01:04.734
-Yeah. Yeah. In two years.

00:01:04.734 --> 00:01:06.367
-You were an old pro
back in the day.

00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:07.434
Is it coming back?
Is it like riding a bike?

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:09.801
-Back in the day, Seth.

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:12.133
Yeah, no audience.
-Yeah.

00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:14.367
-But I'm excited. This is great.

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:15.667
I'm excited.
-Yeah.

00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:17.300
-How does this feel for you?

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:18.701
-I'm used to it now.

00:01:18.701 --> 00:01:21.834
I worry more, to be honest,
like how it is for guests

00:01:21.834 --> 00:01:23.434
who are used to coming out
to wild applause.

00:01:23.434 --> 00:01:27.167
Like I now would spook I think
if there were human beings here.

00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:28.634
-Oh, really?

00:01:28.634 --> 00:01:31.634
-I'm going to be like, "Eww."
-But you get that excitement.

00:01:31.634 --> 00:01:32.868
It's a little different.

00:01:32.868 --> 00:01:34.667
-Yeah, I think when
an audience comes back,

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:37.467
the adrenaline will elevate.
-Yeah.

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:41.501
I can talk to anybody
anytime anywhere.

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:45.100
-Yeah. Well, the interesting
thing about it is like --

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.133
and I want to ask because you
and your brother, Ollie,

00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:48.701
have a podcast.
-Yeah, "Sibling Revelry."

00:01:48.701 --> 00:01:50.701
-"Sibling Revelry." And you've
been doing it for a while.

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:55.634
And so much of the conversations
we hear now, especially

00:01:55.634 --> 00:01:58.334
conversations we hear between
celebrities, are podcast form.

00:01:58.334 --> 00:02:00.567
So you are hearing
more intimate conversations

00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:02.100
with people than we
ever did before.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.934
So audiences are less -- People
don't notice when they're gone.

00:02:06.934 --> 00:02:08.601
-It's true.

00:02:08.601 --> 00:02:11.334
I also think that
actually doing a podcast

00:02:11.334 --> 00:02:14.200
has helped me
in doing things like this.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.334
Do you know what I mean? When
you're on the other side of it,

00:02:17.334 --> 00:02:20.567
I just can't believe
how hard it was to get into

00:02:20.567 --> 00:02:23.033
the rhythm of
being the interviewer,

00:02:23.033 --> 00:02:25.267
like what the actual balance is.

00:02:25.267 --> 00:02:27.968
And then I do all the editing
and Oliver just like --

00:02:27.968 --> 00:02:29.868
I was going to say [bleep] off.

00:02:29.868 --> 00:02:32.534
We'll bleep that.
[ Laughs ]

00:02:32.534 --> 00:02:34.834
-I like that you're like,
"I'm so good at it now.

00:02:34.834 --> 00:02:36.467
I never say things
like [bleep] off.

00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:37.868
Just go back."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.868 --> 00:02:40.033
-Okay, cut, cut, no.

00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:43.234
But Oliver just, like, leaves
and I have to edit everything.

00:02:43.234 --> 00:02:46.434
So in the beginning,
the editing process was insane

00:02:46.434 --> 00:02:48.501
'cause we were so, you know --

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:50.601
-Was it cross-talk?
Was that the biggest issue?

00:02:50.601 --> 00:02:53.868
-Oh, cross-talk.
Just long-winded.

00:02:53.868 --> 00:02:55.667
-Yeah.
-You know, we're not --

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:58.868
We're not -- I'm used to
answering the questions.

00:02:58.868 --> 00:03:01.734
So I would go on and on for like
five minutes about something.

00:03:01.734 --> 00:03:04.601
I'd be like,
"Just cut all of this."

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:07.868
-Did you ever do them in person?

00:03:07.868 --> 00:03:09.501
Or were they always --
even before the pandemic.

00:03:09.501 --> 00:03:11.868
-In person, we did.
And I miss doing them in person.

00:03:11.868 --> 00:03:14.033
-Yeah.
-And we would go for hours.

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:17.000
It would be like 3 1/2 hours.
We'd have so much fun.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.868
And then one time we decided to
bring wine into the equation.

00:03:20.868 --> 00:03:23.167
And that was the last time
we did that.

00:03:23.167 --> 00:03:25.200
I was like,
"This is a terrible idea."

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.667
-And it's a pretty cool
podcast idea.

00:03:26.667 --> 00:03:28.767
They should know that you guys
talk to other siblings.

00:03:28.767 --> 00:03:29.934
You talk to people
about siblings.

00:03:29.934 --> 00:03:30.934
-We got to get you
and your sibling on.

00:03:30.934 --> 00:03:32.133
-We'd do it in a heartbeat.

00:03:32.133 --> 00:03:34.234
-Yeah, because it's really fun.

00:03:34.234 --> 00:03:36.067
And you realize
it's a relationship

00:03:36.067 --> 00:03:38.701
that people don't really talk
about a lot,

00:03:38.701 --> 00:03:40.801
even though everyone
loves their siblings.

00:03:40.801 --> 00:03:43.734
But it's probably the most
important relationship

00:03:43.734 --> 00:03:45.400
that we have.
-Yeah.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.300
-Nobody knows you
like your sibling, good or bad.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.701
It's the most complicated
relationship

00:03:50.701 --> 00:03:53.667
and even more than probably
your parents.

00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:56.667
You know, there's nobody
who understands the way

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:58.033
you were raised
like your sibling does.

00:03:58.033 --> 00:03:59.067
-I agree.

00:03:59.067 --> 00:04:02.033
-And if it's a good
relationship,

00:04:02.033 --> 00:04:04.434
it's just the most beautiful
thing you can have in your life.

00:04:04.434 --> 00:04:06.434
And if it's a challenging
relationship,

00:04:06.434 --> 00:04:09.200
I think what I've learned is
that people long for it.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.467
They long to have
that sibling connection.

00:04:11.467 --> 00:04:13.300
-So I want to ask, too,
because you have kids.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:15.167
And having an important
relationship

00:04:15.167 --> 00:04:17.534
with a sibling like you do,
I have the same.

00:04:17.534 --> 00:04:20.567
Like now, I'm so desperate
for them to have it.

00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:24.267
And I realize --
My sons get along really well.

00:04:24.267 --> 00:04:25.701
But there's also a thing
as a parent,

00:04:25.701 --> 00:04:27.100
you can't be in charge of
how they get along.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:28.534
They're either going to
like each other or not.

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:30.467
-You just get really dark
when they're fighting.

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:32.133
"One day I'll be dead!"
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:32.133 --> 00:04:33.567
-"And you'll only have
each other!"

00:04:33.567 --> 00:04:37.167
-"You'll only have each other!"

00:04:37.167 --> 00:04:40.901
I want to ask real quick
about -- I heard your oldest --

00:04:40.901 --> 00:04:42.701
Bing is 10.
-Bing is 10.

00:04:42.701 --> 00:04:44.400
-And I'm always fascinated
what kids are into.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.100
Bing is into something that I
don't think I've heard yet.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.267
-Oh, yeah.
-For a 10-year-old.

00:04:47.267 --> 00:04:50.534
-Yeah, he actually loves playing
the stock market.

00:04:50.534 --> 00:04:52.400
-Okay.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.133
Which I don't know how
this happened.

00:04:54.133 --> 00:04:56.000
-Is it like Fisher-Price
stock market or the real one?

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:57.834
-Oh, no, no, no,
the real stock market.

00:04:57.834 --> 00:05:00.067
Like, he wants stocks
for his birthday.

00:05:00.067 --> 00:05:01.000
It was a thing.
-Wow.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.801
-Yeah. And he follows them.

00:05:02.801 --> 00:05:06.534
He wakes up. He looks on his
apps and follows his stocks.

00:05:06.534 --> 00:05:08.534
And he makes good money.

00:05:08.534 --> 00:05:10.934
-Oh, he's using --
This has happened?

00:05:10.934 --> 00:05:12.467
[ Laughter ]
You've got him --

00:05:12.467 --> 00:05:15.367
You bought him stocks?
-Yes, he loves his stocks.

00:05:15.367 --> 00:05:16.734
And Foot Locker is his favorite

00:05:16.734 --> 00:05:18.767
because it really does well
apparently.

00:05:18.767 --> 00:05:20.901
I don't really follow Foot
Locker, but Bing tells me.

00:05:20.901 --> 00:05:22.200
-Okay.
-You know.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.801
And then there was one day
where it kind of dipped

00:05:23.801 --> 00:05:25.033
and he was a little worried.

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:26.501
[ Laughter ]

00:05:26.501 --> 00:05:27.934
-Well, that's a thing with kids.

00:05:27.934 --> 00:05:30.067
You got to teach them, like,
there's going to be dips.

00:05:30.067 --> 00:05:31.133
[ Laughter ]

00:05:31.133 --> 00:05:32.467
The market's going to have dips.

00:05:32.467 --> 00:05:33.868
-It's wild, honey.

00:05:33.868 --> 00:05:36.667
But it's really interesting
because

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:39.100
he's got a great math brain,

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:43.501
which I just can't relate to,
really, in any way.

00:05:43.501 --> 00:05:45.334
-Same, yeah.
-I just didn't --

00:05:45.334 --> 00:05:48.701
But he's, like, this creative
with a math brain,

00:05:48.701 --> 00:05:51.267
which I'm sure there's
a lot of those out there.

00:05:51.267 --> 00:05:54.868
I don't know many of them
but my son.

00:05:54.868 --> 00:05:58.667
And so he'll go in and he'll
create and draw and drum.

00:05:58.667 --> 00:06:00.767
And then he checks his stocks.

00:06:00.767 --> 00:06:02.501
It's sort of like, what?

00:06:02.501 --> 00:06:04.133
-He's a real renaissance man.
Yeah.

00:06:04.133 --> 00:06:08.100
-I can't wait to see what
Bing Bellamy is going to do

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:12.734
when he gets older,
but I'm encouraging it.

00:06:12.734 --> 00:06:16.200
I wrote on my Instagram,
I was like he'll be handling

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.200
my portfolio in like two years.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:21.801
-So, another son, Ryder.

00:06:21.801 --> 00:06:24.033
-Ryder. He's almost 18.

00:06:24.033 --> 00:06:26.367
-Okay, so he's giving you
a hard time about --

00:06:26.367 --> 00:06:28.434
-Everything.
-And it seems like --

00:06:28.434 --> 00:06:30.400
We edited some stuff together
here because he gives you

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:32.434
a hard time about your sort of

00:06:32.434 --> 00:06:33.834
embracing of
the wellness culture.

00:06:33.834 --> 00:06:35.834
-Oh, yeah.
Well, and just the fact

00:06:35.834 --> 00:06:38.767
that I Instagram
all of my products.

00:06:38.767 --> 00:06:40.901
-Yeah.
-Yeah. So he sort of makes fun.

00:06:40.901 --> 00:06:43.868
-I guess if you had a mom who's
gonna Instagram her products,

00:06:43.868 --> 00:06:45.334
you're making it pretty easy
for an 18-year-old.

00:06:45.334 --> 00:06:47.067
Let's just look at
what Ryder did.

00:06:47.067 --> 00:06:49.067
-Oh, God.
-Hi, guys.

00:06:49.067 --> 00:06:50.701
Kate Hudson here.

00:06:50.701 --> 00:06:54.100
I know you know me
because you follow me.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:55.467
[ Chuckles ]
I'm so funny.

00:06:55.467 --> 00:07:00.801
Today, we're going to be making
goji berry

00:07:00.801 --> 00:07:03.667
in bloom protein shake.

00:07:03.667 --> 00:07:05.734
It's delicious.
I give it to my kids.

00:07:05.734 --> 00:07:08.801
They love it.
My whole family loves it.

00:07:08.801 --> 00:07:10.367
[ Laughter ]
-There's my little comedian.

00:07:10.367 --> 00:07:13.334
-Fairly accurate?
Is it fairly accurate?

00:07:13.334 --> 00:07:15.000
-Yeah. It's fairly accurate.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.734
I mean, he's also deeply funny.

00:07:17.734 --> 00:07:22.934
Like, Ryder -- I mean, he
clearly is going to probably

00:07:22.934 --> 00:07:25.100
follow in the footsteps of --

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:28.734
I would say he's probably
the funniest in our family.

00:07:28.734 --> 00:07:32.033
So this is like
every day for me.

00:07:32.033 --> 00:07:33.634
Nobody makes me laugh
like Ryder.

00:07:33.634 --> 00:07:36.400
And he just constantly makes fun
of me.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:38.400
-I also want to talk about
the show real quick.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:39.434
-Oh, yeah.
-This is really cool.

00:07:39.434 --> 00:07:41.834
This is season two,
Octavia Spencer,

00:07:41.834 --> 00:07:44.367
star of it
and also the executive producer.

00:07:44.367 --> 00:07:45.701
And so she actually --

00:07:45.701 --> 00:07:48.400
Did she reach out to you,
for bringing you onboard?

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.200
-She did.
Also, Reese is a producer on it,

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:52.801
Witherspoon,
with Hello Sunshine,

00:07:52.801 --> 00:07:54.667
so they both did.

00:07:54.667 --> 00:07:57.934
And it was kind of -- It was
wild because I get this call.

00:07:57.934 --> 00:07:59.033
First, it was actually Reese,

00:07:59.033 --> 00:08:00.667
and she's like,
"What are you doing?"

00:08:00.667 --> 00:08:02.100
And I was like,
"What do you mean?

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:05.300
It's a pandemic. I'm home.
I'm literally in my house."

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:07.701
And she's like, "Do you want to
come do this show

00:08:07.701 --> 00:08:10.300
in about 3 1/2 weeks?"

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:13.868
And in my mind, I was,
like, in -- It was, like,

00:08:13.868 --> 00:08:15.734
in the middle of everything
that was happening.

00:08:15.734 --> 00:08:17.300
Kind of freaked me out
a little bit.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:19.767
But then I was like, "I'm not
really doing anything."

00:08:19.767 --> 00:08:22.934
And I felt like Apple's going to
have all of our backs.

00:08:22.934 --> 00:08:25.634
If Octavia is doing it, I would
die to work with Octavia.

00:08:25.634 --> 00:08:27.334
And then I spoke with Octavia.

00:08:27.334 --> 00:08:29.667
I mean, I just fell madly
in love with her.

00:08:29.667 --> 00:08:33.133
She's the greatest,
and we had a blast.

00:08:33.133 --> 00:08:37.968
We had a real blast,
had a very close-knit family

00:08:37.968 --> 00:08:39.467
because nobody
could do anything

00:08:39.467 --> 00:08:41.200
but be with each other,
you know?

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:42.501
And it was great.

00:08:42.501 --> 00:08:44.334
-Well, that's really cool.
Congrats on the show.

00:08:44.334 --> 00:08:45.434
And it's just so lovely
to see you in person.

00:08:45.434 --> 00:08:47.868
-I know.
-Thank you for making us

00:08:47.868 --> 00:08:48.968
your first show
back in a studio.

00:08:48.968 --> 00:08:50.901
-Oh, I'm so happy to be here.

00:08:50.901 --> 00:08:53.801
-The second season of "Truth Be
Told" is available on Apple TV+.

